**Sec. 70501.** This section terminates the previously-owned clean vehicle credit by amending Section 25E(g) to change the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to September 30, 2025.

**Sec. 70502.** This section terminates the clean vehicle credit for vehicles acquired after September 30, 2025, by amending Section 30D(h) and making conforming changes to Section 30D(e).

**Sec. 70503.** This section terminates the qualified commercial clean vehicles credit by amending Section 45W(g) to change the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to September 30, 2025.

**Sec. 70504.** This section terminates the alternative fuel vehicle refueling property credit by amending Section 30C(i) to change the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to June 30, 2026.

**Sec. 70505.** This section terminates the energy efficient home improvement credit for property placed in service after December 31, 2025, and amends Section 25C(d)(2)(C) to require oil furnaces or hot water boilers to meet 2021 Energy Star criteria and be rated for fuel blends with at least 20% eligible fuel.

**Sec. 70506.** This section terminates the residential clean energy credit for expenditures made after December 31, 2025, by amending Section 25D(h) and removing phase-out provisions in Section 25D(g).

**Sec. 70507.** This section terminates the energy efficient commercial buildings deduction for property with construction beginning after June 30, 2026, by adding a new termination subsection to Section 179D.

**Sec. 70508.** This section terminates the new energy efficient home credit by amending Section 45L(h) to change the expiration date from December 31, 2032, to June 30, 2026.

**Sec. 70509.** This section terminates special cost recovery rules for energy property by amending Section 168(e)(3)(B)(vi) to remove specific subclauses, applying to property with construction beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70510.** This section modifies the zero-emission nuclear power production credit by prohibiting credits for specified foreign entities immediately and for foreign-influenced entities two years after enactment, as defined in new Section 7701(a)(51).

**Sec. 70511.** This section accelerates the termination of the clean hydrogen production credit by amending Section 45V(c)(3)(C) to change the expiration date from January 1, 2033, to January 1, 2028.

**Sec. 70512.** This section terminates the clean electricity production credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and imposes strict restrictions on prohibited foreign entities, including detailed definitions for "specified foreign entity" and "foreign-influenced entity" with specific ownership and control thresholds. It also denies credits for wind and solar leasing arrangements and extends the statute of limitations for errors related to material assistance from prohibited foreign entities to six years.

**Sec. 70513.** This section terminates the clean electricity investment credit for wind and solar facilities placed in service after December 31, 2027, and imposes restrictions on prohibited foreign entities, including a recapture rule for payments to such entities within ten years of placing property in service. It also modifies domestic content requirements, eliminating the energy credit for certain property and applying the credit to qualified fuel cell property at a fixed 30% rate.

**Sec. 70514.** This section phases out the advanced manufacturing production credit for applicable critical minerals (other than metallurgical coal) starting in 2031, terminating it for wind energy components after December 31, 2027, and for metallurgical coal after December 31, 2029. It adds metallurgical coal as an applicable critical mineral with a 2.5% credit rate and imposes restrictions on prohibited foreign entities regarding material assistance.

**Sec. 70515.** This section restricts the extension of the advanced energy project credit program by amending Section 48C(e)(3)(C) to state that the credit amount "shall not be increased," effective immediately upon enactment.

**Sec. 70521.** This section extends the clean fuel production credit to December 31, 2029, and modifies it by prohibiting foreign feedstocks (requiring fuel to be derived exclusively from feedstocks produced in the U.S., Mexico, or Canada) and prohibiting negative emission rates, except for fuels derived from animal manure. It also coordinates the credit with the sustainable aviation fuel credit, eliminates the special rate for sustainable aviation fuel, and extends the small agri-biodiesel producer credit to December 31, 2026, with an increased rate of 20 cents per gallon.

**Sec. 70522.** This section restricts the carbon oxide sequestration credit by prohibiting credits for specified foreign entities and foreign-influenced entities, and establishes parity for different uses of qualified carbon oxide by setting a base credit of $17 per metric ton (inflation-adjusted after 2026) for secure geological storage, tertiary injection, or utilization.

**Sec. 70523.** This section amends the calculation of adjusted financial statement income for excess tax base corporations by allowing intangible drilling and development costs to be taken into account, specifically by reducing the income measure by depreciation and section 263(c) expenses and disregarding related financial statement depreciation and depletion, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70524.** This section expands the definition of qualifying income for publicly traded partnerships to include income from hydrogen storage, carbon capture, advanced nuclear, hydropower, and geothermal energy, allowing these entities to avoid the 4% excise tax under Section 7704, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70525.** This section creates a new Section 6435 allowing the Treasury to pay individuals who remove eligible indelibly dyed diesel fuel or kerosene from a terminal, refunding the previously paid excise tax, effective for fuel removed 180 days after enactment.

**Sec. 70531.** This section modifies the de minimis entry privilege for commercial shipments by imposing a civil penalty of up to $5,000 for the first violation and $10,000 for subsequent violations of customs law, effective 30 days after enactment, and repeals the commercial shipment exception effective July 1, 2027.

**Sec. 70601.** This section makes the limitation on excess business losses for noncorporate taxpayers permanent and adjusts the threshold amounts for calculating excess business losses by updating the base year from 2018 to 2025 and the inflation adjustment year from 2017 to 2024.

**Sec. 70602.** This section amends Section 707(a)(2) to clarify that payments from partnerships to partners for property or services are treated as compensation for services or payments for property, rather than distributions, effective for services performed or property transferred after enactment.

**Sec. 70603.** This section amends Section 162(m) to apply the $1,000,000 deduction limit on executive compensation to controlled groups of publicly held corporations, requiring the allocation of the limit among group members based on the proportion of remuneration provided to specified covered employees.

**Sec. 70604.** This section imposes a 1% excise tax on remittance transfers funded with cash, money orders, cashier's checks, or similar physical instruments, collected by the remittance transfer provider, while excluding transfers funded from regulated financial institution accounts or U.S.-issued debit/credit cards, effective for transfers made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70605.** This section imposes a $1,000 penalty on "COVID-ERTC promoters" who fail to comply with due diligence requirements for Employee Retention Credit claims, limits credits and refunds to those filed by January 31, 2024, and extends the assessment period for ERTC-related deficiencies to six years.

**Sec. 70606.** This section requires taxpayers to include their Social Security Number (and the SSN of any dependent for whom tuition is claimed) and the Employer Identification Number of the educational institution to claim the American Opportunity Tax Credit or Lifetime Learning Credit, effective for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70607.** This section appropriates $15,000,000 for the Department of the Treasury to deliver a report to Congress within 90 days on the cost and feasibility of replacing the IRS Direct File program with public-private partnerships for free tax filing, including taxpayer preferences and cost assessments for a new free direct e-file system. [end of text]


